From 19eece1f4c49e689a23b8b2a61d411ea691e00fe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sureshkumar <suresure@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Mon, 27 Jul 2026 12:40:56 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] prplMesh: improve MLO client metrics, association handling,
 and identity tracking
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

This change addresses several gaps in Multi-Link Operation (MLO) client
support across the agent, controller, and HAL layers, improving metrics
reporting, client identification, association handling, and topology
representation.

1. Per-link metrics reporting

Enhance AP Metrics Response generation for MLO clients by reporting both
aggregate MLD-level statistics and per-link statistics. Per-link traffic
counters, signal measurements, and PHY rates are now exposed for each
affiliated link, ensuring standards-compliant reporting and accurate
visibility into link-level performance.

The HAL client statistics and connection notifications are extended to
carry MLO identity and affiliated-link information, allowing per-link
metrics to be populated from association time onward.

2. MLO-only affiliated metrics reporting

Affiliated STA Metrics information is now generated only for MLO
clients. A thread-safe MLO client registry shared across monitoring and
management components tracks MLO affiliation state and prevents
non-compliant reporting for non-MLO stations.

3. Association frame persistence and fallback handling

Improve association frame availability for MLO clients by introducing a
persistent fallback mechanism when management frames arrive after client
connection notifications. The fallback lookup now correctly resolves
frames using affiliated link addresses and shared association-frame
storage, ensuring capability queries can retrieve association data
reliably.

Additional fixes correct link-address matching issues that previously
prevented fallback lookups from succeeding.

4. Non-fatal capability parsing failures

Client capability processing now stores the raw association frame and
capability information before attempting frame parsing. Parsing failures
are treated as warnings rather than fatal errors, ensuring capability
data remains available even when newer or partially supported MLO
elements cannot be decoded.

5. Duplicate notification suppression

Prevent duplicate processing of a single MLO client caused by multiple
per-link connection notifications. A short-term deduplication mechanism
suppresses redundant topology notifications and capability requests
while preserving normal behavior across reconnects.

6. Correct radio identification for MLO associations

Fix radio resolution during MLO association handling by using link-level
identifiers rather than MLD-level identifiers, ensuring association
frames are attached to the correct radio instance.

7. MLD identity caching

Cache observed AP and client MLD addresses as they become available and
use them to populate incomplete MLD configuration information when later
updates arrive without valid addresses. Explicitly provided addresses
continue to take precedence over cached values.

8. Legacy client fallback for MLD identity

Add a fallback mechanism for environments where the client MLD address
is not reported by the underlying wireless stack. In such cases, the
interface address is used as the client MLD identity.

9. Affiliated link identity cleanup

Rename a misleading affiliated backhaul link field whose contents
represent a station address rather than a BSSID, improving readability
and reducing ambiguity throughout the codebase.

10. MLD topology representation

Add MLD Structure reporting to topology responses. Separate structures
are generated for access-point and backhaul MLD entities, including MLD
identifiers and affiliated-link membership information.

11. Test and simulation enhancements

Extend the dummy HAL and test infrastructure to support MLO simulation,
allowing MLO-specific behavior to be exercised without EHT-capable
hardware. Add coverage for affiliated-link metrics reporting as part of
the default test suite.

Overall, this change improves standards compliance, ensures accurate
per-link visibility for MLO clients, strengthens association and
capability handling, and enhances controller awareness of MLD topology
and identity information.

Bug fixes:
Handle out-of-order AP/STA MLD TLVs by auto-creating missing AP MLD4entries,
remove invalid APMLDMAC DM updates, and validate reported MLO link-metric BSSIDs
against all affiliated-link BSSIDs to prevent6dropping valid metrics reports.
Additional prpl bug fixes
fix MLO STA association, per-link metrics, and vendor event lifecycle

    1. Duplicate topology notifications on MLO VAPs: All three per-band access
       point manager processes received the same STA connection event and all
       three tried to handle it, producing duplicate notifications with wrong
       BSSIDs. Fix: parse the link identifier from the event and skip processing
       in any access point manager that does not own that link interface.

    2. Stale non-MLO entry blocking correct classification: The duplicate-
       detection logic compared only the BSSID, not the MLO flag. On first
       delivery the STA was recorded as non-MLO (Multi-Link Element not yet
       parsed); on second delivery the check matched on BSSID alone and discarded
       the corrected entry. Fix: include the MLO flag in the check; when it
       differs, update the existing entry in place.

    3. Wrong association frame attributed to MLO STA: The BSSID-only fallback
       for resolving a cached association frame was ambiguous — any recently-
       associated STA on the same BSS left a matching entry. Fix: parse the
       Multi-Link Element from the raw frame bytes to extract the MLD MAC and
       verify it matches before accepting the candidate.

    4. STA MLD vendor events never fired: The controller created the STA MLD
       data model instance but never fired the StaMLDAdded event, so qmesh-lib
       never inserted the STA MLD into its database and the steering application
       always reported "STA MLD not found in DB". Fix: fire StaMLDAdded after
       first instance creation, mirroring the AP MLD pattern. The removal path
       is updated symmetrically to fire StaMLDRemoved before deleting the instance.

    5. Per-link signal and traffic counters always zero: The monitor queries the
       kernel using the MLD MAC, but the kernel's station table is keyed by the
       affiliated link MAC, so the query returned nothing. Fix: strip the
       "_link<N>" suffix from the BSS interface name to get the parent MLD
       network interface, then query the kernel on that interface using the
       affiliated link MAC. RCPI is computed from the returned signal strength
       using the standard 802.11 encoding. The aggregate traffic stats
       deduplication set is moved outside the per-BSS loop to prevent the same
       MLD STA being collected three times across a three-BSS AP MLD.

Signed-off-by: Sureshkumar <suresure@qti.qualcomm.com>
